 Plan A doesn't work.", "domain": "MANAGE", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 2, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_006", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Why backups help", "body": "Plans change. Restaurants run out of things. Parties are unpredictable. A backup can keep one food problem from taking over the whole event."}, {"title": "2 | Backups can look different", "body": "A backup might be a snack, food you brought, eating before an event, a second restaurant option, or knowing you can participate without eating."}, {"title": "3 | Backup ≠ failure", "body": "Using your backup means the plan worked exactly as a backup is supposed to work."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_012", "source_evidence": "Celiac Disease Foundation; Meyer & Naveh self-management framework", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_006", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "MANAGE", "subtitle": "A backup gives you options when Plan A doesn't work.", "estimated_minutes": "2 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_012", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "Your Backup Plan Doesn't Mean You Failed", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "A backup is a tool for flexibility, not evidence that you failed.", "try_this": "For your next event, name one Plan A and one easy backup.", "source_evidence": "Celiac Disease Foundation; Meyer & Naveh self-management framework", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_012 — Road Trip, Nothing Nearby", "source_url": "https://celiac.org/dining-and-social-eating/", "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "A backup is a tool for flexibility, not evidence that you failed.", "next_step": "For your next event, name one Plan A and one easy backup.", "related_content_ids": ["252835c7-0b38-52d2-a405-804979b4d391"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": "https://celiac.org/dining-and-social-eating/", "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "8c2513ca-e294-509d-b878-f34785edd138", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_006 Your Backup Plan Doesn't Mean You Failed", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "78bc8dd3-a073-56c7-9db0-8d69a73f8eb4", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "Planning Without Spending Forever Planning", "subtitle": "Good planning should help you live—not take over your life.", "domain": "MANAGE", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_007", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Planning has a purpose", "body": "Planning ahead can make daily activities easier and support decision-making, flexibility, and problem-solving."}, {"title": "2 | Use a minimum useful plan", "body": "Ask: Where am I going? What food question actually matters? Who can help? What's my backup? You may not need to research every possible detail."}, {"title": "3 | Know when to stop", "body": "Once you have enough information for your decision and a backup if needed, more searching may not add much. Your goal is to get back to the activity."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_014", "source_evidence": "Meyer & Naveh (2021) self-management framework; teen-facing extension", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_007", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "MANAGE", "subtitle": "Good planning should help you live—not take over your life.", "estimated_minutes": "2–3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_014", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "Planning Without Spending Forever Planning", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Plan enough to be prepared—then go live the plan.", "try_this": "Set yourself a short planning window for one low-stakes event and identify the three things you actually need to know.", "source_evidence": "Meyer & Naveh (2021) self-management framework; teen-facing extension", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_014 — Everyone Changed Restaurants", "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.3390/nu13051401", "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Plan enough to be prepared—then go live the plan.", "next_step": "Set yourself a short planning window for one low-stakes event and identify the three things you actually need to know.", "related_content_ids": ["2e2e2694-889d-53fd-a23f-f599f0b35b22"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.3390/nu13051401", "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "78bc8dd3-a073-56c7-9db0-8d69a73f8eb4", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_007 Planning Without Spending Forever Planning", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "8a6fb397-7115-565e-82e2-c277c6269321", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "How to Explain Celiac in 20 Seconds", "subtitle": "Clear beats complicated.", "domain": "SPEAK", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 2, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_008", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Start with the essential point", "body": "You can say: “I have celiac disease, so I need to eat gluten-free for medical reasons.”"}, {"title": "2 | Add what matters for the situation", "body": "At a restaurant you might add: “I also need to avoid cross-contact during preparation.” With a friend, you may only need the first sentence."}, {"title": "3 | You control the length", "body": "You can give the 10-second version, explain more, or say you don't want to get into it. Self-advocacy does not require telling your whole medical story."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_010", "source_evidence": "NIDDK factual basis + teen self-advocacy extension", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_008", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "SPEAK", "subtitle": "Clear beats complicated.", "estimated_minutes": "2 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_010", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "How to Explain Celiac in 20 Seconds", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Explain enough to communicate what you need. The rest is your choice.", "try_this": "Write your own 10–20 second celiac explanation in words that sound like you.", "source_evidence": "NIDDK factual basis + teen self-advocacy extension", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_010 — How Much Do I Have to Explain?", "source_url": "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease", "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Explain enough to communicate what you need. The rest is your choice.", "next_step": "Write your own 10–20 second celiac explanation in words that sound like you.", "related_content_ids": ["cb10e2e5-7e72-58f8-8b4b-ad5a14fe2e5a"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": "https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease", "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "8a6fb397-7115-565e-82e2-c277c6269321", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_008 How to Explain Celiac in 20 Seconds", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "5958f945-d7ae-54a6-ba39-42cfd527736f", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "Asking Without Apologizing", "subtitle": "A medical need is not a personal inconvenience you created.", "domain": "SPEAK", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 2, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_009", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Notice the apology reflex", "body": "It can be easy to start with “Sorry, but...” when asking about food. You can be courteous without apologizing for having celiac disease."}, {"title": "2 | Try neutral language", "body": "Examples: “Could you tell me how this is prepared?” “I have celiac disease and need this to be gluten-free.” “Could we check one thing before I order?”"}, {"title": "3 | Clear + kind works", "body": "Being assertive does not mean being rude. Clear information can make it easier for other people to help."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_006", "source_evidence": "Celiac Disease Foundation dining guidance + teen self-advocacy extension", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_009", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "SPEAK", "subtitle": "A medical need is not a personal inconvenience you created.", "estimated_minutes": "2 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_006", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "Asking Without Apologizing", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "You can be polite, clear, and unapologetic about a medical need.", "try_this": "Replace one “Sorry...” script with a neutral question you could actually use.", "source_evidence": "Celiac Disease Foundation dining guidance + teen self-advocacy extension", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_006 — Your Friend's Mom Cooked for You", "source_url": "https://celiac.org/dining-and-social-eating/", "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "You can be polite, clear, and unapologetic about a medical need.", "next_step": "Replace one “Sorry...” script with a neutral question you could actually use.", "related_content_ids": ["3f076ccb-6266-5cb7-ad14-5772e7b9e213"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": "https://celiac.org/dining-and-social-eating/", "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "5958f945-d7ae-54a6-ba39-42cfd527736f", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_009 Asking Without Apologizing", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "8836e66d-b2d9-56ed-992e-051c618d9751", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "It's Gluten-Free, But Do I Have Enough Information?", "subtitle": "“GF ingredients” and “enough information for me to decide” are different questions.", "domain": "TRUST", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_010", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Start with what you know", "body": "Maybe the host says the ingredients are gluten-free. That's useful information."}, {"title": "2 | Ask what's still unknown", "body": "Do you need to know how it was prepared? Whether utensils or serving areas were shared? Whether there is a package or another reliable source? The missing information depends on the situation."}, {"title": "3 | Make your decision", "body": "You can ask more, choose a different option, use a backup, or decide not to eat. The app should help you identify information—not pressure you toward a particular food choice."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_021", "source_evidence": "Beyond Celiac cross-contact guidance + teen lived-experience extension", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_010", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "TRUST", "subtitle": "“GF ingredients” and “enough information for me to decide” are different questions.", "estimated_minutes": "3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_021", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "It's Gluten-Free, But Do I Have Enough Information?", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Trust is not blind reassurance. It's having a process for deciding what information you need.", "try_this": "Practice the sentence: “Thanks. Can I ask how it was prepared?”", "source_evidence": "Beyond Celiac cross-contact guidance + teen lived-experience extension", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_021 — It's Gluten-Free, But I Don't Feel Safe", "source_url": "https://www.beyondceliac.org/gluten-free-diet/cross-contact/", "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Trust is not blind reassurance. It's having a process for deciding what information you need.", "next_step": "Practice the sentence: “Thanks. Can I ask how it was prepared?”", "related_content_ids": ["06e79ecb-5cf9-5147-bf70-a80c7cc37762"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": "https://www.beyondceliac.org/gluten-free-diet/cross-contact/", "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "8836e66d-b2d9-56ed-992e-051c618d9751", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_010 It's Gluten-Free, But Do I Have Enough Information?", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "32952693-e1c7-5074-9903-2811bb919dba", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "When You've Asked the Questions and Still Feel Unsure", "subtitle": "Information and feelings can both be part of the moment.", "domain": "TRUST", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_011", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Check for a real information gap", "body": "Is there a specific unanswered question about ingredients, preparation, or serving? If yes, you can ask it."}, {"title": "2 | Notice if the information question is finished", "body": "Sometimes you have received the information you wanted and still feel nervous. Noticing that difference can help you understand what you're dealing with."}, {"title": "3 | You still own the choice", "body": "You can choose another food, use a backup, not eat, or make the decision that feels appropriate to you. My C-Day should never make eating the “brave” answer."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_024", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; clinical/expert review required", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_011", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "TRUST", "subtitle": "Information and feelings can both be part of the moment.", "estimated_minutes": "3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_024", "review_status": "clinical_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "true", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "When You've Asked the Questions and Still Feel Unsure", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Confidence is not forcing yourself to eat. It's being able to notice what you know, what you don't know, and what you want to do next.", "try_this": "Ask yourself: “Am I missing information, or am I feeling uncertain even though I have the information?”", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; clinical/expert review required", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_024 — I Asked Everything—and I'm Still Nervous", "source_url": null, "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": "Do not diagnose anxiety, disordered eating, or another condition. Do not pressure exposure or eating. Preserve the gentle referral language when distress or restriction is significant."}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Confidence is not forcing yourself to eat. It's being able to notice what you know, what you don't know, and what you want to do next.", "next_step": "Ask yourself: “Am I missing information, or am I feeling uncertain even though I have the information?”", "related_content_ids": ["7b172bbc-678f-5a2f-a028-d66759ed5400"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "32952693-e1c7-5074-9903-2811bb919dba", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_011 When You've Asked the Questions and Still Feel Unsure", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "995b79fc-ee5e-5937-bbb1-c33d2ccc8e8d", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "When Food Feels Different After Diagnosis", "subtitle": "Your relationship with food can change, not just your grocery list.", "domain": "LIVE", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_012", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Food can carry new meaning", "body": "A food you once loved may now look unappealing, feel risky, or remind you of something you cannot safely eat."}, {"title": "2 | Don't turn the reaction into a score", "body": "You do not need to want gluten-containing food, and the app should not make “overcoming” disgust or worry into a challenge."}, {"title": "3 | Pay attention to your overall eating and well-being", "body": "The goal is to have enough foods you can enjoy and to feel supported. If food-related fear or avoidance is significantly limiting eating or causing substantial distress, talk with a parent/caregiver and your celiac healthcare team."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_025", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; clinical review required", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_012", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "LIVE", "subtitle": "Your relationship with food can change, not just your grocery list.", "estimated_minutes": "2–3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_025", "review_status": "clinical_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "true", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "When Food Feels Different After Diagnosis", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Adjustment to celiac can be emotional as well as practical.", "try_this": "Name one gluten-free food or meal that feels enjoyable, easy, or normal to you now.", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; clinical review required", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_025 — Food I Used to Love Looks Gross Now", "source_url": null, "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": "Do not diagnose anxiety, disordered eating, or another condition. Do not pressure exposure or eating. Preserve the gentle referral language when distress or restriction is significant."}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Adjustment to celiac can be emotional as well as practical.", "next_step": "Name one gluten-free food or meal that feels enjoyable, easy, or normal to you now.", "related_content_ids": ["08faa2e2-500e-53fd-b5bf-59792d5df000"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "995b79fc-ee5e-5937-bbb1-c33d2ccc8e8d", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_012 When Food Feels Different After Diagnosis", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "338f77b9-dbd3-50c1-9840-bd2983215ce2", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "I Feel Like a Burden", "subtitle": "Needing consideration isn't the same as causing a problem.", "domain": "LIVE", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_013", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Why the feeling can show up", "body": "Restaurant choices, parties, travel, and group food plans may sometimes change because you need a gluten-free option. That can create guilt even when nobody is blaming you."}, {"title": "2 | Look at what your friends are actually doing", "body": "Friends who help choose a workable restaurant or ask what you need may be trying to include you—not telling you that you are difficult."}, {"title": "3 | You still have choices", "body": "Sometimes you'll suggest a different place. Sometimes you'll eat beforehand. Sometimes you'll ask the group to help. There is no single correct social strategy."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_017", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; expert review required", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_013", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "LIVE", "subtitle": "Needing consideration isn't the same as causing a problem.", "estimated_minutes": "2–3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_017", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "I Feel Like a Burden", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Your needs are real. Inclusion is not something you have to earn by making yourself invisible.", "try_this": "Think of one person you could tell: “Here's what actually makes food plans easier for me.”", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; expert review required", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_017 — Everyone Always Chooses Where I Can Eat", "source_url": null, "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Your needs are real. Inclusion is not something you have to earn by making yourself invisible.", "next_step": "Think of one person you could tell: “Here's what actually makes food plans easier for me.”", "related_content_ids": ["1e73b6de-bb0d-5274-9fc8-33a3a6af9ed5"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "338f77b9-dbd3-50c1-9840-bd2983215ce2", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_013 I Feel Like a Burden", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "abbe40a9-fbf5-5c5b-a966-0f2f7bf28252", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "Celiac Fatigue Is Different From Not Knowing What to Do", "subtitle": "You can know exactly what to do and still be tired of doing it.", "domain": "LIVE", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_014", "cards": [{"title": "1 | The work adds up", "body": "Checking labels, researching restaurants, explaining celiac, planning backups, and thinking ahead can take mental energy."}, {"title": "2 | Skill doesn't erase effort", "body": "Being experienced with celiac doesn't mean every decision becomes effortless. You can be good at self-management and still be tired of managing."}, {"title": "3 | Reduce the load when you can", "body": "Use familiar options, repeat plans that work, ask other people to help, or choose a lower-effort strategy when appropriate."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_020", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; expert review required", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_014", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "LIVE", "subtitle": "You can know exactly what to do and still be tired of doing it.", "estimated_minutes": "2–3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_020", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "Celiac Fatigue Is Different From Not Knowing What to Do", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Planning fatigue is not the same as failure.", "try_this": "Identify one celiac task someone else could help you with this week.", "source_evidence": "Teen lived-experience synthesis; expert review required", "related_practice": "PRACTICE_020 — I'm Just Tired of Thinking About Celiac", "source_url": null, "review_note": "Keep this lesson flagged for expert review before public release. The lesson translates evidence and/or teen lived experience into teen-facing self-management content.", "clinical_safety_note": null}}, "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": "Planning fatigue is not the same as failure.", "next_step": "Identify one celiac task someone else could help you with this week.", "related_content_ids": ["2e67569d-a3ca-530e-9f28-6b7c43a2d810"], "xp_value": 5, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "IN_REVIEW", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": false, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": true}, "placeholder": {"content_id": "abbe40a9-fbf5-5c5b-a966-0f2f7bf28252", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "QL_014 Celiac Fatigue Is Different From Not Knowing What to Do", "subtitle": null, "domain": null, "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": null, "intro": null, "content_body": [], "choices": [], "feedback": {}, "teaching_point": null, "next_step": null, "related_content_ids": [], "xp_value": 0, "source_type": "EDITORIAL", "source_organization": null, "source_title": null, "source_url": null, "author": null, "review_status": "DRAFT", "expert_reviewer": null, "medical_review_required": true, "content_version": 1, "last_reviewed": null, "active": false}}, {"record": {"content_id": "59f84298-c5d8-5f4d-8e00-e00b714e48e8", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "title": "From Speaking Up to Advocating", "subtitle": "Advocacy can be small, practical, and optional.", "domain": "LEAD", "topic": null, "interaction_type": "KNOWLEDGE", "difficulty": null, "estimated_minutes": 3, "intro": null, "content_body": {"format": "quick_learn_v1", "content_id": "QL_015", "cards": [{"title": "1 | Start with self-advocacy", "body": "Self-advocacy is asking for the information, accommodations, or support you need."}, {"title": "2 | Advocacy can grow outward", "body": "You might suggest that school event food information be shared earlier, help a friend understand cross-contact, correct a myth, or support someone newly diagnosed."}, {"title": "3 | You don't owe the world a lesson", "body": "Having celiac does not make you responsible for educating everyone. Choosing not to advocate in a particular moment is also a valid choice."}], "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_028", "source_evidence": "Meyer & Naveh self-management framework + teen-facing advocacy extension", "expert_reviewed": false, "original_metadata": {"content_id": "QL_015", "content_type": "QUICK_LEARN", "domain": "LEAD", "subtitle": "Advocacy can be small, practical, and optional.", "estimated_minutes": "3 min", "xp_value": "+5", "related_practice_id": "PRACTICE_028", "review_status": "expert_review", "expert_reviewed": "false", "medical_review_required": "false", "content_version": "1.0", "active": "true", "title": "From Speaking Up to Advocating", "source_type": null, "key_takeaway": "Leadership can mean making something better—but advocacy is an option, not another celiac obligation.", "try_this": "Think of one small change that would make a place you spend time more celiac-incl